WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.805
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online IELTS preparation.

2
00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:08.468
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:16.842
Unit five: Listening: Numbers, Spelling and Distractors. Today you will write numbers, dates and names correctly and avoid common traps.

4
00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:19.852
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.114
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:25.789
Digit.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:29.584
Digit means a single number from 0 to 9.

8
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:31.904
The code has six digits.

9
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.983
Your turn. Say: Digit.

10
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:38.718
Digit.

11
00:00:39.483 --> 00:00:40.148
Double.

12
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.985
Double means the same letter or number twice.

13
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:46.780
Double seven means 77.

14
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:49.455
Your turn. Say: Double.

15
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.080
Double.

16
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:54.887
Currency.

17
00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:58.254
Currency means the money used in a country.

18
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.420
The currency is pounds.

19
00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:03.495
Your turn. Say: Currency.

20
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:07.265
Currency.

21
00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:09.006
Percentage.

22
00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:12.283
Percentage means a part out of one hundred.

23
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:15.347
A high percentage of students pass.

24
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.660
Your turn. Say: Percentage.

25
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.478
Percentage.

26
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:24.253
Capital letter.

27
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.712
Capital letter means a large letter used at the start of names.

28
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:31.421
Write names with a capital letter.

29
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:34.878
Your turn. Say: Capital letter.

30
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:38.885
Capital letter.

31
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:40.407
Hyphen.

32
00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:43.904
Hyphen means a short line joining words.

33
00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:46.261
Write well-known with a hyphen.

34
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.270
Your turn. Say: Hyphen.

35
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:52.914
Hyphen.

36
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:54.407
Trap.

37
00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:57.973
Trap means something that makes you choose wrongly.

38
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:00.037
Watch out for the trap.

39
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.012
Your turn. Say: Trap.

40
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:06.647
Trap.

41
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:08.431
Double-check.

42
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:11.788
Double-check means to check again carefully.

43
00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:13.837
Double-check your spelling.

44
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.963
Your turn. Say: Double-check.

45
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:20.766
Double-check.

46
00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:25.287
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.893
Visal, what number traps appear in Listening?

48
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:41.273
The speaker may say thirteen and thirty, or fifteen and fifty. Listen to the stress: thirTEEN has a strong end, while THIRty has a strong start.

49
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:44.162
How do I write dates and money?

50
00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:54.880
Write the numbers you hear, such as 15 March or 40 pounds. Do not add extra words if the answer box already shows the unit.

51
00:02:55.983 --> 00:02:57.971
What about spelling mistakes?

52
00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:09.712
A correct word with a wrong spelling loses the mark. Practise common words like accommodation and Wednesday, and write names with capital letters.

53
00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:12.843
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:13.683 --> 00:03:17.993
Grammar focus: Strategy: listen, write, double-check.

55
00:03:18.293 --> 00:03:19.469
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:23.344
ThirTEEN and THIRty sound different because of the stress.

57
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.061
A wrong spelling means no mark, even if the word is correct.

58
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:37.762
Practise number pairs like thirteen and thirty. Learn to spell common IELTS words. In the last minute, double-check spelling, capitals and the word limit.

59
00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:42.284
Be careful! Don't say: I write thirteen for thirty.

60
00:03:44.233 --> 00:03:47.487
Say: I listen to the stress: THIRty.

61
00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:49.264
Check number stress.

62
00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:52.823
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.134
Booking a Hotel Room.

64
00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:58.083
A guest books a room for the fifteenth of March.

65
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.237
The receptionist says the price is thirty pounds a night.

66
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:07.787
The guest asks if it is thirteen pounds, and the receptionist corrects her.

67
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.168
The guest spells her name: M, A, double L, O, R, Y.

68
00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:17.304
At the end she repeats the phone number digit by digit.

69
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.460
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:23.317 --> 00:04:25.328
Which number has the stress at the end?

71
00:04:25.628 --> 00:04:29.003
A, thirty. B, three. C, thirteen.

72
00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:34.531
The answer is C: thirteen.

73
00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:37.358
'Double seven' means blank.

74
00:04:37.658 --> 00:04:42.263
A, 77. B, 7. C, 7.7.

75
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.252
The answer is A: 77.

76
00:04:49.417 --> 00:04:52.124
A word with a wrong spelling is usually blank.

77
00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:56.716
A, ignored. B, marked wrong. C, marked correct.

78
00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:02.615
The answer is B: marked wrong.

79
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.390
What is the price per night?

80
00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.318
A, thirteen pounds. B, fifty pounds. C, thirty pounds.

81
00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:16.516
The answer is C: thirty pounds.

82
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.429
What should you do in the last minute?

83
00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:23.789
A, double-check. B, stop. C, guess new answers.

84
00:05:27.333 --> 00:05:29.600
The answer is A: double-check.

85
00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.451
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:35.317 --> 00:05:38.895
Which number or spelling mistakes do you want to avoid in the test?

87
00:05:47.683 --> 00:05:49.126
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:49.426 --> 00:06:05.478
I want to avoid mixing up numbers like thirteen and thirty, so I listen carefully to the stress. I also practise spelling common words, and I write names with capital letters. At the end I double-check every answer.

89
00:06:05.778 --> 00:06:07.511
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:08.417 --> 00:06:13.670
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: listen, write, double-check.

91
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.327
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

92
00:06:19.627 --> 00:06:22.175
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

93
00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:25.348
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

